The local RGB check detected all 20 parked-blocker target interventions in each engine. Each engine also yielded 13 eligible placed pairs. Each engine supplied 132 eligible second-repeat or distractor-only controls. None triggered a false change. The remaining 28 of 160 planned negative comparisons per engine lacked an eligible target patch. They provide no specificity measurement. Across all 80 distractor manipulations per engine, whole-image change exceeded both same-state repeats in 74 Isaac cases and 72 MuJoCo cases.

Both engines missed the declared partial-view yield. The shelf can in PAC01 provided three fully exposed placed views, one fully hidden view and one narrow sliver. At the sliver, the target occupied just three native image columns in MuJoCo, with 74 of 6,155 isolated target pixels visible (@sliver). A 7 × 7 patch could not cover the required 25 target pixels. The other three layouts each qualified under the strictly-between-zero-and-one visibility rule. Their successful partly visible sites exposed about 48.4%, 1.9% and 97.8% of the retained silhouette. Only the first falls within a descriptive 10–90% band. We therefore use the intervention labels for physical state and the exact-input test for certified ambiguity. General semantic support on partial views remains unmeasured.

#figure(image("figures/calibration-sliver.png",width:100%), caption:[Same fixed-camera crop in the MuJoCo calibration. The exposed part of the retained can lies just past the panel edge. The last panel shows evaluator-only instance pixels at native resolution. The verifiers receive no query mask.]) <sliver>

A native-image recount identified an ambiguity in the patch tie break. Indexing the midpoint of a half-open box directly or shifting it by half a pixel changed seven local contrast values across the two engines. No positive pair or qualification verdict changed. The supplement gives both reconstructions. In a separate matched-repeat diagnostic, five completely hidden calibration pairs per engine had zero visible target pixels. At inference resolution, median whole-image absolute change across opposite states was 0.321 intensity levels in Isaac Sim. The median same-state repeat difference on these same pairs was 0.326. Both medians were zero in MuJoCo. Similar magnitudes describe render variation. Establishing statistical indistinguishability in Isaac would require additional repeats and a separate decision test.
